13.5  fstab and mount options

TL;DR
  - /etc/fstab lists what to mount at boot, one line per filesystem: what,
    where, type, options, then two numbers.
  - Name disks by UUID=… (from lsblk -f), not /dev/sdb1: device names can change
    from one boot to the next.
  - Test a new line before rebooting: sudo mount -a mounts everything listed
    that isn't mounted yet.

      lsblk -f
      sudo mount -a
      sudo findmnt --verify      # checks fstab for mistakes

On your machine
  Entries   / (btrfs, subvol=@,noatime) from
            UUID=3f1c2a9e-8b7d-4e21-9c55-0a6b2d7e9f10 · mounted
            /boot/efi (vfat, umask=0077) from UUID=7A2C-1F3B · mounted
            none (swap, defaults) from /swapfile · swap
            /mnt/nas photos (nfs, noauto,nofail) from nas:/photos · NOT mounted

Pitfalls
  - A wrong line can stop the boot in emergency mode. Add nofail for disks that
    may be missing, such as USB drives and network shares.
  - After editing fstab, run sudo systemctl daemon-reload: systemd turns each
    line into a mount unit, and keeps the old ones until then.
  - Network shares (nfs, cifs) wait for the network at boot; with nofail and
    x-systemd.automount, a missing server doesn't block it.
  - Most systems already mount with relatime, which updates access times at most
    once a day: noatime saves little more.

Deep dive

  ▾ What do the two numbers at the end mean?

    The fifth field was for dump, an old backup tool: use 0. The sixth sets the
    order of filesystem checks at boot: 1 for the root filesystem, 2 for the
    others, 0 to skip. btrfs and XFS check themselves, so they use 0.

  ▾ Who reads fstab today?

    On systemd systems, a generator turns every line into a .mount unit at boot
    and at each daemon-reload. That's why systemd options work there:
    x-systemd.automount mounts on first access, and x-systemd.device-timeout=10s
    limits how long boot waits for a disk.

Related
  Commands  mount, findmnt, lsblk, blkid
  Paths     /etc/fstab
  Hub       atfq disk Disk
  See also  atfq 13.6 /dev and udev
  See also  atfq 22.1 Units and timers
